\label{thm:shared-event-quotient}
Let \(E,F\) be events in two contexts \(c,d\in\mathcal{C}\) with \(E\sim F\). Assume the shared-event relation is admissible on the relevant fixed comparison object and is stable under the local Boolean operations there. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item if \(E_1\sim F_1\) and \(E_2\sim F_2\), then \(E_1\cup E_2 \sim F_1\cup F_2\);
    \item if \(E\sim F\), then \(E^{\complement_c}\sim F^{\complement_d}\), where complements are taken in \(A_c\) and \(A_d\) respectively.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, the quotient obtained by identifying shared events is compatible with the local Boolean structure carried by the contexts.

